  "content": {
    "id": "p9-q13-root",
    "type": "question",
    "label": "13",
    "prompt": "If the roots of the equation $x^2+px+q=0$ are consecutive numbers, show that $p^2=4q+1$.",
    "layout": "list",
    "columns": null,
    "questionDiagrams": [],
    "children": [],
    "answer": {
      "short": "$2\\alpha+1=-p$ and $\\alpha^2+\\alpha=q$. Substituting $\\alpha=\\frac{-p-1}{2}$ gives $p^2-1=4q$, hence $p^2=4q+1$.",
      "worked": "Let the roots be $\\alpha$ and $\\alpha+1$.\n\nSum of roots:\n$$2\\alpha+1=-p\\qquad(1)$$\nProduct of roots:\n$$\\alpha^2+\\alpha=q\\qquad(2)$$\nRearrange (1): $\\alpha=\\frac{-p-1}{2}$. Substitute into (2):\n$$\\begin{aligned}\\left(\\frac{-p-1}{2}\\right)^2+\\frac{-p-1}{2}&=q\\\\\\frac{p^2+2p+1}{4}+\\frac{-2p-2}{4}&=q\\\\p^2-1&=4q\\\\p^2&=4q+1\\end{aligned}$$",
      "solutionDiagrams": []
    },
    "answerSpaceMm": 50
  },
